<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- The entitlements for the IPA that a re-signing installer takes over: SideStore,
	     AltStore, LiveContainer. Deliberately NOT src/ios/Cemu.entitlements, which is
	     what the ad-hoc signed TrollStore IPA gets instead.

	     Cemu.entitlements asks for com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit,
	     dynamic-codesigning and com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection.
	     Those are restricted: they only take effect through a provisioning profile that
	     grants them, and a re-signer whose profile does not authorise an entitlement
	     drops the entitlements rather than failing. Including get-task-allow, which is
	     the one every JIT enabler reads off the installed app to decide whether to list
	     it at all. So asking for the full set here would cost the app the single
	     entitlement that actually matters to it - an app that asks for too much ends up
	     indistinguishable from an app that asked for nothing.

	     That is not a guess. It is exactly what v4.5 did with
	     com.apple.developer.kernel.extended-virtual-addressing, and StikDebug stopped
	     listing MuffinEMU entirely; the reasoning is written out at the bottom of
	     Cemu.entitlements. This file is the same lesson applied to the sideload path:
	     ask for the minimum that survives re-signing.

	     On the TrollStore side none of this applies - nothing re-signs that IPA, so it
	     carries the full set and keeps it. -->

	<!-- The one that has to survive. A JIT enabler lists apps by reading get-task-allow
	     off the installed app, and this IPA used to ship unsigned, so it had no
	     entitlements dict at all and a re-signer merging the app's existing entitlements
	     had nothing to merge. The app installed, ran, and was simply never offered for
	     debugging. The build step verifies this key specifically rather than trusting
	     that signing worked. -->
	<key>get-task-allow</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Raises the memory iOS allows before terminating the app, which matters most on
	     6 GB devices like the A12Z iPad Pro where the default sits close to what a retail
	     title needs. Unrestricted, so it costs nothing if a re-signer's profile does not
	     carry it: it is dropped on its own, quietly, without taking get-task-allow with
	     it. That is why the build step reports what was embedded but only REQUIRES
	     get-task-allow. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
